  I’ll inspect the prompt and translator first, then shape both the Python implementation and a deliberately small K semantics around the exact AST constructs this task needs. After that I’ll compile, execute representative cases, run every proof command, and keep `prove.sh` as the reproducible record.
  The task is the “keep only values whose total input frequency is one” problem. I’m choosing a compact list-comprehension implementation; that keeps the source faithful to the contract and limits the semantic surface to function definition/call, list iteration, `count`, comparison, and name lookup. The proof will remain symbolic over an arbitrary integer list, not just the examples.
  The generated program now runs under the new semantics and produces `[1, 3, 4]`, `[]`, and `[0, 2]` for representative duplicate, empty, and negative-input cases. I’m moving to the proof layer now: `verification.k` will define the contract independently as a recursive stable filter over the original input, while `spec.k` will connect the exact translated AST to that reference function.
  The proof needed one semantic refinement: list iteration must remain visible as K transitions so the prover can induct on a symbolic list. I’ve made that change without specializing the data—the iterator still evaluates the translated generator, predicate, and element expressions—and the concrete behavior is unchanged. The symbolic proof is running now.
  The prover is actively consuming CPU rather than stuck on an unsupported term, but the combined two-claim run is exploring more branches than it should. I’m giving this attempt a short window; if it does not close, I’ll isolate the inductive lemma and end-to-end theorem so I can identify and remove the source of branching.
  The isolated run exposed the cause cleanly: the induction hypothesis was too narrow because the iterator deliberately leaves a pending “emit this element” continuation on the K cell. I’m generalizing the lemma over both an arbitrary continuation and an arbitrary output suffix; that is the standard strengthening needed for this continuation-based traversal.
